krightarrow o_j;\\,x)\\,\\mathcal U(o_j),$$\n\nwhere $x$ is the observation history and \"$\\hookrightarrow$\" is a \"decision hypothetical\" connective. Joyce's representation theorem says that expected-utility theories can differ only in the \"epistemic perspective\" they use to evaluate actions. In the paper's operational language, the agent holds a world-model $M$, builds for each action $a$ a transient object $M^{a\\hookrightarrow}$ (a hypothetical), and computes a value from it. The authors stress that hypotheticals are \"decision-theoretic instruments constructed from beliefs,\" comparable to \"notes on scratch paper,\" and not themselves beliefs.\n\nThe three theories differ in how they build the hypothetical:\n\n- **EDT** (Eq. 2): $\\arg\\max_a \\mathbb E[V\\mid \\mathrm{Obs}=x,\\mathrm{Act}=a]$. It conditions, so every correlation in $P$ survives.\n- **CDT** (Eq. 3): $\\arg\\max_a \\mathbb E[V\\mid \\mathrm{do}(\\mathrm{Act}=a),\\mathrm{Obs}=x]$. It performs Pearl surgery on the physical action node, so only causal consequences of the act change.\n- **A-FDT** (Eq. 4):\n\n$$\\boxed{\\;\\mathrm{A\\text{-}FDT}(P,G,x)\\;:=\\;\\arg\\max_{a\\in\\mathcal A}\\;\\mathbb E\\Big[V\\;\\Big|\\;\\mathrm{do}\\big(\\mathrm{fdt}(\\underline P,\\underline G,\\underline x)=a\\big)\\Big]\\;}$$\n\nThe summary line from the paper is \"FDT is to subjunctive dependence as causal decision theory is to causal dependence.\" A CDT agent intervenes on the token \"my action is $a$.\" An A-FDT agent intervenes on the *type*, the output of the decision function.\n\n## 3. Unpacking Equation (4)\n\n**Inputs.** The inputs are a probability distribution $P$ over the model's variables, a graph $G$, and an observation $x$. $G$ is the key object. Like Pearl's causal graph, it says which variables would change if a given node were intervened on. Unlike Pearl's graph, its edges encode all \"decision-relevant subjunctive dependencies\": causal, logical, computational, and predictive. The paper requires $G$ to be supplied as input, \"in the same way that the relevant causal structure is given as an input to CDT.\"\n\n**The intervened node.** $P$ contains a variable $\\mathrm{fdt}(\\underline P,\\underline G,\\underline x)$ representing the output of the very procedure being executed, on exactly these inputs. The underlines mean \"dequoting\": the variable's name is built from the data $P, G, x$. This is self-referential, but the paper argues the self-reference is harmless. The agent need not know the *value* of $\\mathrm{A\\text{-}FDT}(P,G,x)$ to have a variable *representing* it, and Kleene's second recursion theorem (\"quining\") shows that such self-representing data structures exist.\n\n**Observation enters by changing the node, not by conditioning.** This is the structural difference from CDT. When the observation changes from $x$ to $y$, CDT changes what it conditions on. A-FDT changes *which node it intervenes on*, from $\\mathrm{fdt}(\\underline P,\\underline G,\\underline x)$ to $\\mathrm{fdt}(\\underline P,\\underline G,\\underline y)$. Each of these is a different logical fact: the output of the same procedure on different inputs. Other observation-indexed nodes remain in the graph, available to be affected by the surgery.\n\n**Surgery.** $\\mathrm{do}(\\mathrm{fdt}(\\cdot)=a)$ sets that node to $a$ and recomputes everything downstream of it in $G$. These are the variables that *subjunctively depend* on the function's output: the agent's own physical action, a predictor's model of her, a twin's action, and the probability that she sees a given observation at all. Everything not downstream of the node keeps its prior distribution. Utility is then averaged over the resulting distribution, and the best $a$ is chosen.\n\n**Why subjunctive dependence rather than causation.** The paper's examples: two calculators computing $6288+1048$ have outputs constrained to agree \"insofar as the calculator made no errors.\" Causal dependence is a special case: a scribe copying a calculator's output depends on it causally and subjunctively. Mere statistical correlation is not subjunctive dependence. Two coincidentally pink calculators do not covary under intervention, so if the scribe had bought a green calculator, the Tokyo calculator would still be pink. A predictor who models the agent's decision function in detail depends on that function. A smoking lesion does not.\n\n**Counterpossibility.** If the function outputs $a_1$ on these inputs, then it does so of logical necessity, so imagining the output $a_2$ is a \"counterpossible.\" The paper accepts that this is the theory's main drawback relative to CDT and notes that humans reason about false logical sentences all the time. Its pragmatic move is to avoid the question by making the effects of the counterpossible *input* to the procedure, as encoded in $G$. The authors also reject the alternative FDT′ (\"What if I used a different decision function?\"), because preserving the predictor's correspondence with the agent would reintroduce the same counterpossible reasoning.\n\n**Agreement with CDT and EDT.** If every correlation between $\\mathrm{Act}$ and other variables is caused by $\\mathrm{Act}$ (the only exception being a dependence on the function node itself), then $\\mathbb E[V\\mid a]=\\mathbb E[V\\mid\\mathrm{do}(a)]=\\mathbb E[V\\mid \\mathrm{do}(\\mathrm{fdt}=a)]$ and all three theories agree. They diverge only where something correlates with the act without being caused by it.\n\n## 4. A step-by-step procedure\n\nThe paper presents Equation (4) as an executable algorithm, and I make it explicit here.\n\n1. Build or receive $(P,G)$: the variables, the stochastic structure, and the dependence graph, including nodes for the function's output on each observation that could have occurred.\n2. Identify the node $\\mathrm{fdt}(\\underline P,\\underline G,\\underline x)$ corresponding to the observation actually made.\n3. For each $a\\in\\mathcal A$, compute $P(\\,\\cdot\\mid\\mathrm{do}(\\mathrm{fdt}(\\underline P,\\underline G,\\underline x)=a))$ by propagating $a$ down the edges leaving the node. Hold non-descendants at their prior values.\n4. Compute $\\mathbb E[V]$ in each hypothetical and output the maximizer.\n\nThe paper warns of a footgun: the *variable* $\\mathrm{fdt}(\\underline P,\\underline G)$ in the model is not the same as the *action* the procedure returns. The first is something the agent manipulates; the second is what comes out.\n\n## 5. Worked examples\n\nAll dollar figures are the paper's utilities. Where the printed text has arithmetic slips (the \"$999,000\" for EDT in Newcomb's problem, \"$110,00\" in the transparent case), I use recomputed values.\n\n### 5.1 Newcomb's problem\n\nThe model has a stochastic node $\\mathrm{Accurate}$ (0.99). The node $\\mathrm{fdt}$ points to $\\mathrm{Act}$ and to $\\mathrm{Prediction}$, and $\\mathrm{Prediction}\\to\\mathrm{Box\\,B}$. Then\n\n- $\\mathrm{do}(\\mathrm{fdt}=\\textit{onebox})$: the act is one-boxing, and $P(\\text{full})=0.99$. $\\mathbb E[V]=0.99\\cdot 1{,}000{,}000=\\$990{,}000$.\n- $\\mathrm{do}(\\mathrm{fdt}=\\textit{twobox})$: the act is two-boxing, and $P(\\text{full})=0.01$. $\\mathbb E[V]=0.01\\cdot1{,}001{,}000+0.99\\cdot1{,}000=\\$11{,}000$.\n\nSo A-FDT one-boxes. No retrocausation is involved. The paper emphasizes that the agent \"just thinks it is foolish to imagine her action changing without also imagining [the function] taking on a different value, since she thinks the predictor is good at reasoning about\" the procedure.\n\nA common objection: if she is nearly certain the box is full, why not take the extra $1,000? The reply is that the question \"what if I two-boxed?\" is *answered by the hypothetical*, and in the hypothetical the function outputs *twobox*, which the predictor's model of her shares.\n\n### 5.2 Twin prisoner's dilemma\n\nBoth actions are children of $\\mathrm{fdt}$. Intervening to *cooperate* gives \\$1,000,000; intervening to *defect* gives \\$1,000. A-FDT cooperates.\n\n*My extension (not in the paper).* Suppose the twin's action copies the function's output only with probability $\\rho$, via a noise node analogous to $\\mathrm{Accurate}$, and otherwise defects. Then\n$\\mathbb E[V\\mid\\mathrm{do}(C)]=10^6\\rho$ and $\\mathbb E[V\\mid\\mathrm{do}(D)]=1000\\rho+1{,}001{,}000(1-\\rho)$ if the twin's non-copy outcome is cooperation. In that case cooperation wins iff $2{,}000{,}000\\rho>1{,}001{,}000$, i.e. $\\rho>0.5005$. This shows how fallible dependence is handled: partial edges, as the paper says, give weakened but not eliminated dependence.\n\n### 5.3 Smoking lesion\n\nThe paper's formalization shows that $G$ must differ from the Newcomb graph even though the probability table is isomorphic. The lesion determines preferences ($\\mathcal U_S$ if she has the lesion, $\\mathcal U_R$ otherwise) and so which of two functions, $\\mathrm{fdt}(\\underline{P_S},\\underline G)$ or $\\mathrm{fdt}(\\underline{P_R},\\underline G)$, controls the act. Neither function node has an edge to $\\mathrm{Cancer}$. Intervening on the $\\mathcal U_S$-function therefore leaves $P(\\text{lesion})=p$ unchanged. Smoking adds $\\$1{,}000$ in the lesion world, so it beats refraining by $p\\cdot\\$1{,}000$, and she smokes. The Newcomb and lesion cases differ in one thing only: whether the downstream correlate (prediction versus cancer) *computes something about the agent's function*.\n\n### 5.4 Transparent Newcomb and observation indexing\n\nThe graph has two nodes, $\\mathrm{fdt}(\\cdot,\\textit{full})$ and $\\mathrm{fdt}(\\cdot,\\textit{empty})$. $\\mathrm{Prediction}$ determines which one controls $\\mathrm{Act}$, because the prediction determines what the agent sees. Suppose she observes a full box and let $q$ be her credence that she two-boxes upon seeing it empty.\n\n- $\\mathrm{do}(\\mathrm{fdt}(\\textit{full})=\\textit{onebox})$: with probability 0.99 the predictor says \"one-box,\" the box is full, and she one-boxes for \\$1,000,000. With probability 0.01 the prediction errs, the box is empty, and the other node decides, giving $1000q$ in expectation. Total: $990{,}000+10q$.\n- $\\mathrm{do}(\\mathrm{fdt}(\\textit{full})=\\textit{twobox})$: with probability 0.99 the predictor says \"two-box,\" so she sees an empty box and the other node decides: $990q$. With probability 0.01 she sees a full box and two-boxes: \\$1,001,000. Total: $10{,}010+990q$.\n\nFor every $q\\in[0,1]$ the first is larger, so A-FDT one-boxes while looking at two full boxes. In the paper's words, \"when she envisions two-boxing, she envisions a scenario where she instead (with high probability) sees that the box is empty.\"\n\nI add one gloss here. Equation (4) as printed contains no conditioning on $\\mathrm{Obs}$. The observation serves only as an index. (The paper's calculation writes \"$\\mathrm{Obs}=\\textit{full}$\" inside the do-expression, but a literal reading, where the observation is conditioned on and so rules out the empty-box worlds, would give back CDT's verdict, so I read it as indexing.) Observation is *not* used to discard worlds in which the agent would have seen something else, and that is the whole point.\n\nThis is also Parfit's hitchhiker: paying in the city is formally the same as one-boxing against a full box, and the paper argues the two should be treated alike.\n\n### 5.5 Blackmail\n\n*Mechanical blackmail.* The blackmailer runs the program iff she predicts the agent would pay on receiving the letter. The graph has the nodes $\\mathrm{fdt}(\\textit{blackmail})$ and $\\mathrm{fdt}(\\emptyset)$, and $\\mathrm{Blackmailer}$ selects which one controls the act. Intervening to *pay* yields a world where she is blackmailed and loses \\$1,000. Intervening to *refuse* yields a world where she is (almost surely) never blackmailed. A-FDT refuses even holding the letter. The paper adds the sensitivity analysis: with prediction-error rate 0.09%, refusing costs $0.0009\\times\\$1{,}000{,}000=\\$900$ in expectation, less than \\$1,000, so she still refuses. With error rate 0.011% the expected cost of refusing is about \\$110, so she pays. (This uses the paper's simplified payoffs: paying always costs \\$1,000.)\n\n*XOR blackmail.* Here the infestation node has no incoming edge from the function. Changing $\\mathrm{fdt}(\\textit{letter})$ changes whether she would pay and the probability of seeing the letter, but not the infestation probability. Since paying does not change the termite probability, paying is a pure \\$1,000 loss and she refuses. EDT pays because conditioning on payment, given the letter, sets the termite probability to zero. CDT also refuses, but for different reasons, and its hypothetical says the predictor \"erred.\"\n\n### 5.6 Death in Damascus\n\nDeath's appointment-book entry depends on the function's output, as does the agent's location. $\\mathrm{do}(\\mathrm{fdt}=\\textit{stay})$ puts both in Damascus: she dies and keeps \\$1,000. $\\mathrm{do}(\\mathrm{fdt}=\\textit{flee})$ puts both in Aleppo: she dies and loses \\$1,000. She stays. There is no instability to resolve, because A-FDT's hypotheticals never imagine Death's location staying fixed while hers moves. The instability the paper attributes to CDT arises from exactly that.\n\n## 6. How A-FDT treats counterfactuals and dependencies\n\nThe theory has a simple counterfactual semantics, summed up in the paper as \"FDT agents preserve all decision-relevant subjunctive dependencies\" in their hypotheticals, whereas CDT preserves only causal structure and EDT preserves every statistical correlation. The authors summarize: \"EDT respects too many action-environment correlations, while CDT respects too few.\"\n\nThree consequences follow.\n\n1. **Hypothetical worlds can contradict the actual one.** An agent who sees a full box evaluates a hypothetical in which she is likely facing an empty one. The paper concedes that both CDT and A-FDT hypotheticals \"contain impossibilities\": CDT agents imagine that predictors fail, while A-FDT agents imagine that they saw different observations. The question is which impossibility tracks the structure the agent believes in.\n2. **Dominance is relative to a notion of hypothetical.** CDT-dominance, A-FDT-dominance (holding all relevant subjunctive dependencies fixed), and EDT-\"dominance\" are each just restatements of the theory's own hypothetical, so none is neutral evidence. The authors prefer utility actually achieved as the arbiter. Under this A-FDT-style dominance, they say, A-FDT agents never take a dominated action.\n3. **No updating on the information that would change the hypothetical.** A-FDT's observation-indexing is the source of the \"updateless\" name of its predecessor, UDT. The paper notes that TDT both conditioned on evidence *and* changed the intervention point, which created \"a kind of 'double update'\" that \"proves harmful in practice.\"\n\n## 7. What the paper leaves open, and a proposed operational reading\n\n**The central gap: where does $G$ come from?** The paper says plainly that \"Figuring out how to deduce the structure of subjunctive dependencies from observation and experiment is perhaps the largest open problem\" and that it is unclear \"whether it can be done in a satisfactory manner within Pearl's framework.\" It also says a more elegant $\\mathrm{true}(\\cdot)$ operator for logical conditionals is not available, which is why $G$ is an input.\n\nWhat follows from the source: A-FDT is well-defined *relative* to a correct $(P,G)$. The paper treats cases where the model \"accurately portray[s] the situation.\" Any verdict depends on $G$ being right.\n\n*Proposed operational heuristic (mine, not the paper's).* To decide whether variable $Z$ should be a descendant of the function node, ask: does $Z$ have a mechanism that *computes, copies, or models the output of this decision procedure* (or a sufficiently accurate model of it), so that if the procedure had returned a different output on that input, $Z$ would have a different value? A twin, a predictor's simulation, and a record produced by one all qualify. A common cause that merely correlates, like the lesion, fails the test, because the lesion fixes preferences and would not change if the function's output did. The paper's test that \"the cancer is not evaluating the agent's future decisions\" is the same idea. I stress that this is a modeling rule for building $G$, not a derivation, and in hard cases (partial simulations, shared training histories, approximate copies) it is only a starting point. A natural device for imperfect dependence is a noise node feeding the child, as the paper does with $\\mathrm{Accurate}$.\n\n**Actions versus policies.** Equation (4) selects an action given $x$. The authors say they actually prefer intervening on a *policy*, a map from observations to actions, which \"makes a difference in certain multi-agent dilemmas.\" Within the single-agent examples above the two approaches agree. My gloss: the action version at $x$ evaluates $a$ while treating the outputs at other observations (the $q$'s above) as given by $P$; the policy version optimizes all outputs jointly. In the examples the $q$ never mattered. That is a lucky feature of those cases, not a general guarantee, and the policy version is the safer reading when others' behavior depends on what you would do in several branches.\n\n**Counterpossible reasoning.** The paper says the lack of a satisfactory account \"is the main drawback of FDT relative to CDT at this time.\" Delegating the work to $G$ is a deferral, and a reader should understand A-FDT as shifting the difficulty onto the model builder.\n\n**Self-referential priors.** $P$ includes beliefs about $\\mathrm{fdt}(\\underline P,\\underline G,\\underline x)$ itself. The paper observes that the choice of prior over one's own output is \"inconsequential\" in its examples and does not rescue EDT-like pathologies, since A-FDT uses the prior only to weight nodes it does not intervene on. Whether this holds in general is not established.\n\n**Fairness.** The paper claims A-FDT matches or beats CDT and EDT in \"fair problems,\" meaning those where beliefs are accurate and the payoff depends only on behavior in the problem at hand (not on the decision procedure used). It admits that no formal criterion exists. Its own footnote gives the counterexample: a problem that punishes anyone who acts as a copy of A-FDT acts. Such a problem is unfair to A-FDT and unfair in a way that is hard to transpose to other theories. The strong claim that no fair dilemma defeats A-FDT is, in the authors' words, one they have \"not been able to construct\" a counterexample to, which is weaker than a theorem.\n\n## 8. The philosophical motivation\n\nThe argument for A-FDT is explicitly *pragmatic*: \"if an agent wants to get as much utility as she can, she should weigh different imaginary worlds in which her decision procedure has different logical outputs, and execute the action corresponding to the best such world.\" The supporting arguments are:\n\n- **Precommitment.** CDT and EDT agents would pay to precommit to one-boxing in the transparent Newcomb problem. A-FDT agents \"simply act as they would have ideally precommitted to act,\" at no cost.\n- **Value of information.** Because A-FDT agents act as they would have precommitted, they do not assign negative expected value to information, whereas EDT agents would pay to be blindfolded.\n- **Reflective stability.** CDT recommends self-modifying away from itself; the paper says A-FDT is \"reflectively stable in fair problems.\"\n- **Utility.** Predictors who can see the agent's procedure will fill the boxes for A-FDT agents and not for CDT or EDT agents. \"Parfit's desert is stacked high with the corpses of such agents.\"\n\nThe paper also answers the charge that A-FDT agents hold a delusion about controlling mathematics or the past. The reply is symmetric: a deterministic CDT agent \"controls\" her token action only in the sense of imagining worlds across which it differs, and A-FDT agents do the same with the type. A principled objection to one would apply to the other.\n\n## 9. Conclusion\n\nA-FDT, Equation (4), is CDT-style surgery relocated to a different node. Instead of intervening on the physical act, the agent intervenes on the output of her own decision function, with the dependence structure $G$ determining what else changes. Observations select *which* function-output node is intervened upon, and they are not conditioned on, which lets the agent reason across worlds where she would have seen something different. This one rule yields cooperation with twins, one-boxing, smoking, paying the driver, and refusing blackmail, and it avoids the instabilities of CDT in Death in Damascus.\n\nIts rigor is conditional on a graph of subjunctive dependencies that the paper does not know how to learn or derive, and on counterpossible reasoning it explicitly leaves as an open problem. The reader who applies A-FDT should therefore think of the work as split in two. The first part is modeling: deciding which variables are computations of, or copies of, the agent's own decision procedure. The second is arithmetic, which is easy once the first is done. The most important thing to check in any new case is whether each correlate of one's action is a *model of one's decision procedure* or merely a *correlate of its inputs*.\n\n## References\n\n- Yudkowsky, E. & Soares, N., \"Functional Decision Theory: A New Theory of Instrumental Rationality,\" arXiv:1710.05060 (source of all numbered equations, quotations and the problem formalizations above).\n- Joyce, J., *The Foundations of Causal Decision Theory* (1999).\n- Pearl, J., *Causality* (2000).\n- Gibbard, A. & Harper, W., \"Counterfactuals and Two Kinds of Expected Utility\" (1978).\n- Dai, W., \"Towards a New Decision Theory\" (2009).","refusal":null,"reasoning":"I'm drafting a roughly 2,400–3,000 word essay on A-FDT, centered on Equation (4), and keeping that name fixed throughout. The other theories, CDT and EDT, get their own names, and I still need to settle how to refer to the paper's \"FDT\" label without blurring it with the assigned theory.\n\nI'm also noting that the paper labels its theory \"FDT,\" so I'll say the source uses that name while keeping \"A-FDT\" for the procedure in Equation (4). Next I'm listing the related variants to cover: FDT′, the policy-based version, the evidential FDT*, TDT, and UDT, and I'm checking that quotations stay faithful. I also see that Equation (4) doesn't condition on the observation directly, which conflicts with the transparent Newcomb calculation, so I'll flag that. The Newcomb figures have a few typos I'll need to correct: Eve's one-box value should be $990,000 and the transparent-box expression works out to 10,010 + 990q.\n\nI'm checking the Newcomb and blackmail numbers in the source against my own arithmetic, and I'll work out the one-box and transparent-box values myself rather than trusting the typos. For the smoking lesion case I'm dropping the specific figures and keeping only the structure. For the imperfect-predictor blackmail case, I'm working through the error rate and what it costs the agent who refuses.\n\nI'm checking the blackmail threshold: a 0.011% error rate works out to about $110, which is below the $1,000 loss from paying, so the agent should refuse to pay in the imperfect-predictor version. I'm also outlining the essay, starting with the naming scope and the setup, then unpacking the inputs to Equation (4).\n\nI'm working through how to unpack the inputs of Equation (4), including the node index and the do-operator surgery, and I plan to lay out an operational recipe for building the subjunctive dependence graph, noting that the source treats that as an open problem. Then I'll walk through the worked examples—Newcomb, smoking lesion, transparent Newcomb with observation indexing, the blackmail variants, and twin prisoner's dilemma—and close by comparing policy and action level, where the observation's dependence on the hypothetical is the key point.\n\nI'm now sketching how to present the action-level versus policy-level distinction, treating the observation's dependence on the hypothetical as the key point. My working gloss is that the action-at-x version holds the other observations' outputs fixed at their prior beliefs, while the policy version optimizes them jointly. I'm marking this as my own addition rather than the paper's claim.\n\nNow I'm moving on to the wrap-up: checking where dominance and fairness fit, which assumptions the approach depends on, how counterpossibles and a misspecified graph could break it, and what the prior over the other observations should look like. After that I'll write the conclusion.\n\nI'm working through the Newcomb case with the accurate-prediction branch, where a one-box choice makes the predictor fill the opaque box. In the inaccurate branch, the outcome depends on how the empty-box observation is handled, so I'm working out the expected value for choosing one box given that.\n\nI'm checking the transparent-box value: with a 0.99 accurate predictor, one-boxing gives about $990,000 plus a small term in q, while two-boxing tops out near $11,000. So one-boxing wins clearly, and the observation only serves to pick out the node, not to filter which worlds count.\n\nI'm sketching how the Death in Damascus case fits the same procedure: the decision node feeds both the act and Death's prediction, so intervening on it to \"stay\" versus \"flee\" shows staying comes out about $1,000 better. Then I'm working through the twin prisoner's dilemma, where setting the shared node to cooperate beats defecting, and I'm noting a possible extension with imperfect correlation between the twins.\n\nI'm working out the threshold for the twin case where the copy only matches with probability ρ: cooperating beats defecting once ρ passes about 0.5005. I'll treat the imperfect correlation as a noise node, the same way the accurate predictor was handled, and flag it as my own extension.\n\n","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"I'm drafting a roughly 2,400–3,000 word essay on A-FDT, centered on Equation (4), and keeping that name fixed throughout. The other theories, CDT and EDT, get their own names, and I still need to settle how to refer to the paper's \"FDT\" label without blurring it with the assigned theory.\n\nI'm also noting that the paper labels its theory \"FDT,\" so I'll say the source uses that name while keeping \"A-FDT\" for the procedure in Equation (4). Next I'm listing the related variants to cover: FDT′, the policy-based version, the evidential FDT*, TDT, and UDT, and I'm checking that quotations stay faithful. I also see that Equation (4) doesn't condition on the observation directly, which conflicts with the transparent Newcomb calculation, so I'll flag that. The Newcomb figures have a few typos I'll need to correct: Eve's one-box value should be $990,000 and the transparent-box expression works out to 10,010 + 990q.\n\nI'm checking the Newcomb and blackmail numbers in the source against my own arithmetic, and I'll work out the one-box and transparent-box values myself rather than trusting the typos. For the smoking lesion case I'm dropping the specific figures and keeping only the structure. For the imperfect-predictor blackmail case, I'm working through the error rate and what it costs the agent who refuses.\n\nI'm checking the blackmail threshold: a 0.011% error rate works out to about $110, which is below the $1,000 loss from paying, so the agent should refuse to pay in the imperfect-predictor version. I'm also outlining the essay, starting with the naming scope and the setup, then unpacking the inputs to Equation (4).\n\nI'm working through how to unpack the inputs of Equation (4), including the node index and the do-operator surgery, and I plan to lay out an operational recipe for building the subjunctive dependence graph, noting that the source treats that as an open problem. Then I'll walk through the worked examples—Newcomb, smoking lesion, transparent Newcomb with observation indexing, the blackmail variants, and twin prisoner's dilemma—and close by comparing policy and action level, where the observation's dependence on the hypothetical is the key point.\n\nI'm now sketching how to present the action-level versus policy-level distinction, treating the observation's dependence on the hypothetical as the key point. My working gloss is that the action-at-x version holds the other observations' outputs fixed at their prior beliefs, while the policy version optimizes them jointly. I'm marking this as my own addition rather than the paper's claim.\n\nNow I'm moving on to the wrap-up: checking where dominance and fairness fit, which assumptions the approach depends on, how counterpossibles and a misspecified graph could break it, and what the prior over the other observations should look like. After that I'll write the conclusion.\n\nI'm working through the Newcomb case with the accurate-prediction branch, where a one-box choice makes the predictor fill the opaque box. In the inaccurate branch, the outcome depends on how the empty-box observation is handled, so I'm working out the expected value for choosing one box given that.\n\nI'm checking the transparent-box value: with a 0.99 accurate predictor, one-boxing gives about $990,000 plus a small term in q, while two-boxing tops out near $11,000. So one-boxing wins clearly, and the observation only serves to pick out the node, not to filter which worlds count.\n\nI'm sketching how the Death in Damascus case fits the same procedure: the decision node feeds both the act and Death's prediction, so intervening on it to \"stay\" versus \"flee\" shows staying comes out about $1,000 better. Then I'm working through the twin prisoner's dilemma, where setting the shared node to cooperate beats defecting, and I'm noting a possible extension with imperfect correlation between the twins.\n\nI'm working out the threshold for the twin case where the copy only matches with probability ρ: cooperating beats defecting once ρ passes about 0.5005. I'll treat the imperfect correlation as a noise node, the same way the accurate predictor was handled, and flag it as my own extension.\n\n","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,"signature":".eyJlbmRwb2ludF9zbHVnIjoiYW50aHJvcGljL2NsYXVkZS1zb25uZXQtNS41LTIwMjYwOTI4fGFudGhyb3BpYyJ9"}]}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":83920,"completion_tokens":11343,"total_tokens":95263,"cost":0.28127,"is_byok":false,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0,"cache_write_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0,"video_tokens":0},"cost_details":{"upstream_inference_cost":0.28127,"upstream_inference_prompt_cost":0.16784,"upstream_inference_completions_cost":0.11343},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":2868,"image_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0}}}